{"t":9555,"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a68943ea59b3de642","run_id":"0mwm27wcf-8b74a236","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPZMyETVUL82LGizRWF8v5","status":"completed","output_file":"C:\\Users\\spesce\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\a3af3560164d967d\\00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a\\tasks\\a68943ea59b3de642.output","summary":"The astrolabe is one of the oldest and most versatile scientific instruments. Its roots lie in ancient Greece, where thinkers built on the geometry of stereographic projection, which maps the celestial sphere onto a flat plane. Hipparchus is often credited with early forms of this idea in the second century BCE. Ptolemy later described related principles, and Theon of Alexandria wrote a treatise on the instrument in the fourth century CE.\n\nThe astrolabe flourished in the Islamic world from the eighth century onward. Scholars such as al-Fazari and Mashallah wrote early works on it. Later astronomers refined the design and added new scales and functions. Instrument makers in Baghdad, Cairo, Isfahan, and al-Andalus produced finely engraved brass examples. Uses grew beyond astronomy. Observers could tell time, find the direction of Mecca for prayer, determine latitude, and survey heights.\n\nKnowledge of the astrolabe reached Christian Europe through Muslim Spain in the tenth and eleventh centuries. By the Middle Ages it was a staple of university study. Chaucer wrote a treatise on it for his young son in the 1390s. Mariners later adapted a simpler form for navigation at sea, though the quadrant and sextant eventually replaced it. Today, surviving astrolabes are prized museum pieces, and they stand as monuments to the work of many cultures.\n\nWORKER_TOKEN=74102958","usage":{"total_tokens":6076,"tool_uses":1,"duration_ms":7386},"uuid":"b9633ba5-8cfa-4b62-be2c-94103a78a5f2","session_id":"00fd7e89-3d8b-462b-a750-92ea96c2d04a"}
